This version was posted on July 28 after roughly two weeks of feedback
from yourself and several others on RGRN (see the Closing Comments at
the end for details). It is the oldest version that I have posted in a
permanent location. This version makes the following statement:
    the conservative method - keep your dungeon level
    roughly equal to your experience level. When you run
    out of monsters on a level, go on down. This improves
    your HP and power (makes you more dangerous) and
    more likely to survive a battle with the weak monsters
    you find on these levels. Many people have had YAFAP
    with this method - it worked for them and can work for
    you. However, if your armor doesn't improve, you can
    also die an early death to the first fast monster you face.
which is structured in the following manner:
 o a title for the method, for reference in discussions
 o a general method of play (in this case - the DL/XL comparison and
moving down when running out of monsters) [what to do]
 o commentary on the expected benefits and cautions [why to do it /
what to avoid]
So within two weeks of the original RGRN message - based on feedback -
I already had moved away from a simple "keep XL=3DDL for a few levels"
to something more complete.

The wording in the "current" version was introduced in the -R2 version
on September 4, 2002 (another month later) based on further feedback.
The change made was to reverse the first two sentences and acknowledge
some use of the pet in killing the monsters before moving down. I did
this to emphasize the more active part of the method of play (the new
first sentence) and push the XL=3DDL statement to the commentary.

Based on that chronology - it appears I was (and still am)
 o open to constructive comments and
 o do not recommend lingering on a level to increase XL as part of a
"starting strategy"

